# LF for everything text, everywhere. Without this, core.autocrlf=true -- the
# Git-for-Windows installer default -- checks the whole repo out with CRLF, and
# .prettierrc pins "endOfLine": "lf", so `npm run format:check` fails on essentially
# every file in the repo. A Windows QA run hit exactly that: ~1363 files reported, and
# the only way past it was setting core.autocrlf=false per clone by hand.
#
# text=auto still leaves genuinely binary files alone, and it does NOT rewrite what is
# already committed -- git only normalises on the way in. All but three tracked files
# were already LF; those three (.editorconfig, .prettierignore,
# public/bridgelink-icon.svg) were renormalised in the same commit as this rule, so
# nobody discovers them later as a surprise diff under `git add --renormalize`.
#
# Note this does not fix an EXISTING Windows clone: the working tree there stays CRLF
# and git reports it clean. Re-clone, or run `git add --renormalize .` after setting
# core.autocrlf=false.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Redundant under the rule above, kept because it says why for shell scripts
# specifically.
scripts/*.sh text eol=lf

# The exceptions. PowerShell and batch scripts ship to Windows; keep CRLF so they are
# not silently normalised to LF by an edit made on macOS or Linux.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Git hooks are executed by `sh`, and a CR at the end of each line makes sh fail with
# "command not found" -- under husky that propagates and BLOCKS THE PUSH. Verified: a CRLF
# copy of the pre-push shim exits 127. core.autocrlf=true is the Git-for-Windows installer
# default, so without this a Windows contributor cannot push at all, and the error points at
# a line number that looks like a broken Node install rather than at line endings.
.husky/** text eol=lf
